module ECL_UNITS.

import ECL_ERRORS.

context Units.
context Nesting.

> This model is normative for the lifecycle of a general ECL unit: successful
> completion with an ordered result stack, failure with an error, and the
> strict nesting of units opened inside another.
>
> It deliberately abstracts over evaluation steps, name resolution, concrete
> values and error dictionaries, environment writes, I/O, randomness, and all
> other non-stack effects. The surrounding language specification governs
> those subjects and requires that unit failure does not roll back effects
> already performed. The model also omits the code a unit runs, the stack it
> begins on, launch and observation policy, task identity, task trees,
> scheduling, awaiting, deadlines, cancellation causes, and reclamation. In
> particular, cancellation reaches this model only as an evaluator-supplied
> failure error, while a waiting operation's timeout does not alter the target
> unit.
>
> `@attempt` begins a unit, waits synchronously for one terminal transition,
> and reifies that outcome. `@spawn` begins the same kind of unit and returns a
> task capability immediately; `task.await` later reifies the attached unit's
> terminal outcome through the same mapping. The two therefore agree on result
> envelope and failure mapping. They differ in ownership: `@spawn` creates a
> task, which owns the tasks spawned inside it, while `@attempt` is a stack
> transaction that owns nothing, so tasks spawned inside it belong to the
> enclosing task or the session root. Task lifetime, ownership, and waiting
> behavior are governed by the concurrency model.
>
> `Begin unit` starts a unit with no parent.
> Submissions and unit constructors differ in how they expose the eventual
> outcome; those policies do not change unit execution semantics. Units that
> running code opens inside another, such as `@attempt`, `@load`, and module
> construction, begin through `Begin nested unit`.
Units ~> Begin unit.
---
> Beginning a unit selects one identity that has never begun and makes it
> active.
some unit: Unit, ~unit-active? unit, ~unit-succeeded? unit, ~unit-failed? unit |
  unit-active?' unit and
  ~unit-succeeded?' unit and
  ~unit-failed?' unit and
  (all other: Unit, other != unit |
    unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
    unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
    unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
    unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
    unit-error' other = unit-error other).

where

> A unit that has begun is in exactly one phase: active, successfully
> completed, or failed. One in no phase has not begun. `unit-stack` is the
> evaluator-supplied result of a successful unit and `unit-error` the error of
> a failed one. Intermediate evaluator stacks are outside the abstraction.
Unit.
{Units} unit-active? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-succeeded? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-failed? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Units} unit-stack unit: Unit, unit-succeeded? unit => Value.
{Units} unit-error unit: Unit, unit-failed? unit => Value.
> Nesting is fixed when a unit begins. A unit without a parent is a
> submission's or a task's; its failure is observed by the Session or through
> `task.await`, never by another unit.
{Nesting} unit-nested? unit: Unit => Bool.
{Nesting} unit-parent unit: Unit => Unit.
{Nesting} unit-reifying? unit: Unit => Bool.
---
> A unit is in at most one phase.
unit-active? unit -> ~unit-succeeded? unit and ~unit-failed? unit.

unit-succeeded? unit -> ~unit-failed? unit.

> A successfully completed unit exposes a list: its result stack.
unit-succeeded? unit -> value-type (unit-stack unit) = list-type.

> A failed unit carries an error value and exposes no stack.
unit-failed? unit -> error-value? (unit-error unit).

> Only a unit that has begun is nested, inside another unit that has begun.
unit-nested? unit ->
  (unit-active? unit or unit-succeeded? unit or unit-failed? unit) and
  (unit-active? (unit-parent unit) or
   unit-succeeded? (unit-parent unit) or
   unit-failed? (unit-parent unit)) and
  unit-parent unit != unit.

> Units nest strictly: the parent of an active nested unit is active.
unit-active? unit and unit-nested? unit -> unit-active? (unit-parent unit).

> A parent succeeds only if every failure nested in it was reified and was
> not a cancellation.
unit-nested? unit and unit-failed? unit and unit-succeeded? (unit-parent unit) ->
  unit-reifying? unit and
  ~cancelled-error? (unit-error unit).

> Initially no unit has begun or is nested.
initially all candidate: Unit |
  ~unit-active? candidate and
  ~unit-succeeded? candidate and
  ~unit-failed? candidate and
  ~unit-nested? candidate.

where

> `Complete unit successfully` accepts the result produced by the omitted
> evaluator, makes the active unit terminal, and retains that result as the
> unit's result stack. The evaluator supplies the result; it is not chosen by
> the ECL operation that launched the unit.
Units ~> Complete unit successfully @ 
  unit: Unit, 
  result: Value, 
  unit-active? unit, 
  value-type result = list-type, 
  (all child: Unit, unit-nested? child, unit-parent child = unit
    | ~unit-active? child),
  (all child: Unit, unit-nested? child, unit-parent child = unit, unit-failed? child
    | unit-reifying? child and ~cancelled-error? (unit-error child)).
---
> Successful completion makes the unit terminal and exposes the evaluator's
> complete result stack.
~unit-active?' unit.
unit-succeeded?' unit.
~unit-failed?' unit.
unit-stack' unit = result.

all other: Unit, other != unit |
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

where

> `Complete unit with failure` accepts the error produced by the evaluator or
> by a facility such as concurrency cancellation. Errors are ordinary ECL
> values satisfying the imported `error-value?` schema. Completion makes the
> active unit terminal and records the error value. The evaluator's partial
> stack is neither retained nor exposed. Every error kind follows this same
> unit transition; causes and concrete error data are outside the model.
Units ~> Complete unit with failure @ 
  unit: Unit, 
  error: Value, 
  unit-active? unit, 
  error-value? error, 
  (all child: Unit, unit-nested? child, unit-parent child = unit | ~unit-active? child).
---
> Failure makes the unit terminal and records the evaluator-supplied error.
~unit-active?' unit.
~unit-succeeded?' unit.
unit-failed?' unit.
unit-error' unit = error.

all other: Unit, other != unit |
  unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
  unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
  unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
  unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
  unit-error' other = unit-error other.

where

> `Begin nested unit` opens a unit inside an active parent unit. Units nest
> strictly: a parent cannot become terminal while a unit nested in it is
> active. A reifying nested unit, as `@attempt` opens, turns its failure into
> data its parent continues with. Any other nested failure propagates, so its
> parent cannot succeed. Cancellation is never reified: a nested unit failed
> by a cancellation error prevents its parent's success whatever boundary
> opened it, so cancellation fails every unit of the cancelled task or root
> from the innermost out.
Units, Nesting ~> Begin nested unit @ parent: Unit, unit-active? parent.
---
> Beginning a nested unit is beginning a unit that also records its parent.
> Whether its boundary reifies failure is the opener's choice, which the model
> leaves open here and holds fixed afterwards.
some unit: Unit, ~unit-active? unit, ~unit-succeeded? unit, ~unit-failed? unit |
  unit-active?' unit and
  ~unit-succeeded?' unit and
  ~unit-failed?' unit and
  unit-nested?' unit and
  unit-parent' unit = parent and
  (all other: Unit, other != unit |
    unit-active?' other = unit-active? other and
    unit-succeeded?' other = unit-succeeded? other and
    unit-failed?' other = unit-failed? other and
    unit-stack' other = unit-stack other and
    unit-error' other = unit-error other and
    unit-nested?' other = unit-nested? other and
    unit-parent' other = unit-parent other and
    unit-reifying?' other = unit-reifying? other).
